WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:05.499 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  Destinee - is only days away and as the  sequel to one of the most successful new  IPs ever it brings its fair share of  pressure to developer Bungie it's a  chance to not only expand on the success  of the first destiny but also to show  that Bungie is willing to adapt and  evolve as fan expectations morph with  each passing month to put it bluntly  destiny - is bungees chance to prove  that the studio can still set new bars  because that's exactly what Bungie has  done it laid the foundations for things  we take for granted today freedom of  movement and first-person shooters and  multiplayer matchmaking in console games  we also might not be waiting for the  Xbox 1x to release if halo hadn't made  the case for Microsoft's Console push 16  years ago and say what you will about  destiny but it has found an audience to  say the least  like planetside or warframe its world  blends first-person shooter elements  with those often consigned to mmo's  Ubisoft chase that formula with the  division and emerged with greater  initial sales than even destiny and then  Bioware the iconic studio lauded for its  story driven rpgs seems to be chasing  the MMO shooter genre with its upcoming  project anthem so given bungees ongoing  impact now seems as good a time as any  to look back at how its games change  things and to remember some of its  greatest triumphs that often go  overlooked today  [Music]  the story of bungees founding has been  told before dozens of times by hundreds  of different people they all paint the  picture of a ragtag group of irreverent  college grads always looking for new  ways to do things although they're  arguably best known for their work on  the Xbox Bungie founders Alex Seropian  and Jason Jones began their work on the  niche Macintosh platform they created  early games such as the top-down shooter  Operation Desert Storm and the RPG  Minotaur the labyrinths of Crete  soon after they realized the value of 3d  graphics and set to work on a port for  Minotaur however the game didn't  translate well into the new perspective  and Bungie pivoted in 1993 it made  pathways into darkness the first-person  shooter that established the company is  a legitimate player in the burgeoning  world of game development from this  point forward Bungie was agile finding  success with its ability to adapt  difficult circumstances with juggernaut  such as doom and System Shock on PC  Bungie released marathon the first in a  trilogy of shooters that would give  Macintosh players their own first-person  tentpole and while it didn't find as big  of an audience as its counterparts  marathon did help establish one of the  genres most important mechanics we take  free look for granted today in most  shooters but when marathon released the  ability to completely control your view  with a mouse was novel in the original  doom you could only move your view from  side to side in 1994 System Shock you  had to toggle into Mouse look others  consigned your aiming controls entirely  to the keyboard marathons freedom of  movement popularized a level of  verticality that's the default now and  some of the best multiplayer map design  makes good use of it games such as  counter-strike overwatch and the  original quake are legends largely  because of marathons precedent  despite marathons oft overlooked  importance it didn't find the audience  Bungie would amass with its later titles  but it's sold well enough for the studio  to continue experimenting it expanded  into the PC market it created myth a  strategy title that garnered a cult  community and Oni a third-person action  game that would later be ported by  Rockstar Games but as the 90s came to a  close Bungie began work on another  project it was said to be a real-time  strategy game similar to bungees earlier  myth but set in a sci-fi universe  however the team decided against this  opting instead to  make a third person shooter using a  similar futuristic setting but Bungie  turned on a dime yet again creating  something completely different than the  original plan  [Music]  helos impact on the world of video games  is pervasive  it's almost omnipresent so much so that  we often forget how much the series  really has changed things throughout its  lifespan for one thing I don't think we  would be nearing the release of the Xbox  1x if it weren't for a halo combat  Evolved the original Xbox may have gone  extinct  along with Microsoft's efforts to push  into the console market at the time if  Bungie hadn't signed the deal to bring  its shooter to the new platform halo  made the Xbox a contender in the console  space and laid the seeds that would make  Microsoft a powerhouse in the gaming  world  in a similar vein Xbox Live might not be  the staple it is today if halo 2 hadn't  become a multiplayer phenomenon when it  released setting the standards for fluid  matchmaking and helping game such as  quake 3 arena to push console online  multiplayer  [Music]  I could go into the countless  innovations Halo introduced to the  shooter genre throughout the years but I  don't have that kind of time here but I  will say that Halo games since the start  have also just been fun and it's  difficult to pinpoint why maybe it's the  way each weapon has a different feeling  or maybe it's the way various enemy  types affect the ebb and flow of combat  or maybe it's the way it uses open  environments to create chaotic battles  but also cramped corridors to introduce  a terrifying foes the moment-to-moment  gameplay is always hard to describe but  I think that's what makes it so good  Longy spent a ridiculous amount of time  making you forget there was a controller  in your hand countless games have  borrowed from halos many iterations  trying to mimic the flow of its  mechanics and pacing of its firefights  to this day there are few shooters I  could point to that have succeeded  though the new doom titanfall 2 and of  course destiny come to mind but more on  that shortly all this is to say that  halo was sort of a miracle it was almost  a real-time strategy game it was almost  a third-person shooter but Bungie made  it work as something else entirely and  it was so good that it legitimized the  Xbox and later Xbox Live and console  online multiplayer across the board  after Halo Reach bungees farewell to the  legendary series it was hard to imagine  where it might go next like many people  I have a complicated relationship with  destiny there are times when I love it  there are times when I hate it I'll call  it boring one day only to finish a  harrowing nightfall strike with friends  than next I don't always feel as if  destiny is respecting my time and yes I  think it can be exploitative in the ways  it's random loot system works back when  he was a GameSpot Danny O'Dwyer did an  episode of the point that I still watch  sometimes comparing vanilla destiny to a  slot machine where you have no control  over your own rewards but regardless I'm  excited for destiny - it sounds like  Bungie knows what it needs to do to make  the series the juggernaut of  hyper-connected fun that it could be and  I'm cautiously optimistic that it'll  pull it off or at least get a little bit  closer because make no mistake destiny  is a phenomenon before the division  usurped it destiny was the most  successful new IP ever selling over 500  million dollars on its first day it took  the pitch-perfect feel of bungees halo  games and place them in an MMO it  connected hundreds of thousands of  players in cooperative strikes six  player raids and a share  social space destiny wasn't the first  game to do this but much like halo  established consoles as a viable  platform for PC quality shooters destiny  makes the strongest case for MMO design  and shooters I'm not sure what Bungie  will do after destiny - if it ever does  decide to leave it behind but throughout  its history Bungie has proven that it  knows how to adapt it knows how to  change and in doing so the studio has  flourished it's one of the few  developers that can claim to have had a  massive impact on video games in general  it found ways to go against the current  without sacrificing quality in the  process we may not always recognize it  right away but bungees influences  everywhere it's just a matter of looking  in the right places  [Music]  hey everyone thanks so much for watching  episode 13 of reboot it's a long time  halo fan I had a lot of fun making this  one and as always we want your input so  in the meantime before our next episode  get your questions in what you think of  this episode are you excited for destiny  too you can hit us up on twitter at  jacob deck or emma hardy and as always  leave a thought in the comments below  and we might feature it on our next  episode we'll see you then  [Music]  you

